          "h": "What Is a German Blocked Account (Sperrkonto)?",
          "body": "A **Sperrkonto** (literally 'blocked account') is a special German bank account that proves you have sufficient funds to support yourself during your studies in Germany. German immigration requires you to show **EUR 11,904 per year** (or EUR 992 per month as of 2025\u201326) before issuing a student visa.\n\nThis amount is NOT a deposit that stays locked forever. Instead, you make monthly withdrawals (typically EUR 992/month) once you've obtained your residence permit and registered at a German university. The remaining balance is released after you complete your degree or leave Germany.\n\nThe blocked account exists to prevent student visa abuse and ensure you can afford tuition, accommodation, and living expenses without becoming a financial burden on the German state.\n\nThink of it as a **trust account**: the German government trusts you have the funds, you trust the account provider will release them monthly, and the account provider trusts you'll withdraw responsibly. It's not a savings account\u2014it's proof of financial stability, pure and simple."
